.TH WAYHUD 5 "2026-09-01" "wayhud" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
wayhud \- configuration file for wayhud(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wayhud/config.toml
.PP
.RI ( ~/.config/wayhud/config.toml
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset.)
.SH DESCRIPTION
The file is TOML. It holds one table,
.BR style ,
whose sub\-tables are named presets; a preset is chosen at run time with
.BR "wayhud \-\-style " \fINAME\fR
and defaults to
.BR default .
.PP
A missing file is not an error \- every preset then consists entirely of the
built\-in defaults listed below. A file that fails to parse
.I is
an error, as is a key the program does not recognise: a misspelt setting is
reported rather than silently ignored.
.PP
Presets do
.B not
inherit from one another. Any field a preset leaves out falls back to the
built\-in default, never to
.BR [style.default] .
This is deliberate \- a preset is fully described by its own block, with no
second table to hold in your head.
.PP
Command\-line flags override the selected preset for that invocation; see
.BR wayhud (1).
.SH VALUE TYPES
.TP
.I colour
Any colour GTK's CSS parser accepts:
.BR #b8bb26 ,
.BR #b8bb26cc ,
.BR "rgba(184,187,38,0.8)" ,
or a named colour such as
.BR green .
.TP
.I font
A Pango font description \- family, optional style words, then size in
points, e.g.
.BR "\(dqLythMono Nerd Font 72\(dq" .
The family must match fontconfig exactly; one that does not exist falls back
to the default font without a word of warning.
.TP
.I pixels
Logical pixels, i.e. before the output's scale factor is applied. On a
.B scale 2
display a value of 64 covers 128 device pixels.
.SH KEYS
All keys below live directly in a
.BI [style. NAME ]
table.
.SS Text
.TP
.B font
.RI ( font ,
default
.BR "\(dqLythMono Nerd Font 72\(dq" )
.br
Typeface and size of the message.
.TP
.B color
.RI ( colour ,
default
.BR \(dq#b8bb26\(dq )
.br
Fill colour of the glyphs.
.TP
.B outline
.RI ( colour ,
default
.BR \(dq#1d2021\(dq )
.br
Colour of the stroke around each glyph. Omit the key entirely to draw the
text flat, with no outline pass at all.
.TP
.B outline_width
.RI ( float ,
.IR pixels ;
default: derived from the font size)
.br
Width of that stroke. Left unset it is the font size divided by 14, which
keeps the visible halo a fixed fraction of the stem thickness across sizes;
a constant that looks like a fine contour at 72pt reads as fake bold at
24pt. Set it to pin an exact width. Must not be negative.
.TP
.B line_align
.RB ( left " | " center " | " right ,
default
.BR left )
.br
How lines are aligned relative to each other inside the text block. This is
independent of where the block itself sits.
.SS Placement
.TP
.B halign
.RB ( start " | " center " | " end ,
default
.BR center )
.br
Horizontal placement of the text block on the output.
.B start
is the left edge,
.B end
the right.
.TP
.B valign
.RB ( start " | " center " | " end ,
default
.BR center )
.br
Vertical placement.
.B start
is the top edge,
.B end
the bottom.
.TP
.B margin
.RI ( integer ,
.IR pixels ;
default
.BR 64 )
.br
Gap between the text block and the edge it is anchored to. An axis set to
.B center
is centred by the compositor and ignores this value.
.SS Timing
.TP
.B timeout_ms
.RI ( integer ,
milliseconds; default
.BR 5000 )
.br
How long the fully revealed text stays up. Measured from the
.I end
of the reveal, not from start\-up, so a slow typewriter does not eat into
the reading time. Capped at 3600000 (one hour): there is no way to dismiss a
HUD early, so an accidental extra digit would strand it on screen.
.TP
.B reveal
.RI ( table ,
default
.BR "{ kind = \(dqtypewriter\(dq, cps = 28, cursor = true }" )
.br
How the text appears. See
.B REVEAL
below.
.TP
.B vanish
.RI ( table ,
default
.BR "{ kind = \(dqcollapse\(dq, ms = 420 }" )
.br
How it goes away. See
.B VANISH
below.
.SS Sound
.TP
.B sound
.RI ( table ,
default
.BR "{ enabled = true, freq = 2100.0, decay_ms = 38.0, gain = 0.22, every = 1 }" )
.br
The typewriter blip. See
.B SOUND
below.
.SH REVEAL
A table with a
.B kind
key selecting the variant.
.TP
.B { kind = "instant" }
The whole message appears at once.
.TP
.BR "{ kind = \(dqtypewriter\(dq, cps = " \fIF\fR ", cursor = " \fIBOOL\fR " }"
Typed out character by character.
.B cps
(float, default 28) is the speed in characters per second; a value of zero
or less is treated as
.BR instant .
.B cursor
(boolean, default true) draws a block caret at the write head, which keeps
blinking while the text is held.
.SH VANISH
A table with a
.B kind
key. Every variant except
.B instant
takes
.B ms
(integer, default 420) for its duration.
.TP
.B instant
Gone on the frame the hold expires.
.TP
.B fade
Alpha to zero.
.TP
.B collapse
CRT power\-off: the text squashes towards its middle line, blooms slightly
wider, washes to white and blinks out.
.TP
.B wash
A soft edge sweeps through the text, erasing as it passes. Takes an extra
.B dir
of
.B down
(default) or
.BR up .
.TP
.B untype
The caret walks back and eats the text. The only vanish that makes a sound,
because it is typing; it gets a caret even when the reveal was instant.
.TP
.B dissolve
The glyphs fall apart into blocks, in an order that is pseudo\-random but
fixed, so the decay is stable rather than flickering.
.SH SOUND
The blip is synthesised at run time rather than loaded from a file. The knob
names match
.BR blyamk (1),
so a sound dialled in there with
.B blyamk \-v
transfers over verbatim.
.TP
.B enabled
(boolean, default
.BR true )
.br
Whether to make any noise at all.
.B \-\-no\-sound
overrides this to false.
.TP
.B freq
(float, Hz; default
.BR 2100.0 )
.br
Base frequency of the blip \- its overall pitch.
.TP
.B decay_ms
(float, milliseconds; default
.BR 38.0 )
.br
Ring\-out time. Longer values turn a click into a tone.
.TP
.B gain
(float, 0\(en1; default
.BR 0.22 )
.br
Peak amplitude of a single blip. Overlapping blips sum, and the mix is
clamped rather than limited, so a high gain at a high
.B cps
will clip.
.TP
.B every
(integer, at least 1; default
.BR 1 )
.br
Blip once every N revealed characters. Whitespace never blips \- a space bar
on a movie terminal does not click, and newlines would stutter.
.SH EXAMPLE
A complete file with every key at its default is shipped as
.I config.example.toml
in the source distribution.
.PP
.RS
.nf
[style.default]
font = "LythMono Nerd Font 72"
color = "#b8bb26"
outline = "#1d2021"
halign = "center"
valign = "center"
timeout_ms = 5000
reveal = { kind = "typewriter", cps = 28, cursor = true }
vanish = { kind = "collapse", ms = 420 }

# Louder, redder, and it does not linger.
[style.alert]
color = "#fb4934"
font = "LythMono Nerd Font 96"
timeout_ms = 8000
vanish = { kind = "wash", ms = 300, dir = "up" }

# Self\-destructing note: types in, sits, clicks its way back out.
[style.spy]
color = "#8ec07c"
reveal = { kind = "typewriter", cps = 18, cursor = true }
vanish = { kind = "untype", ms = 900 }

# Small, cornered, silent \- for something that just needs to be on screen.
[style.corner]
font = "LythMono Nerd Font 24"
halign = "end"
valign = "end"
margin = 32
reveal = { kind = "instant" }
vanish = { kind = "fade", ms = 200 }
sound = { enabled = false }
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wayhud (1),
.BR pango-markup (7)
